1082:| The hosted model is down or its reply is cut off (offline lock off) | `cma run` behaves as today (exit 4); the pack never depends on a model | R53 |
1084:| The case root is inside OneDrive or another synced folder | A warning before the case is created, recorded in the case log; a sync client that later restores the lock files is the interference above | R32, R42 |
1112:- Requirements (revision 17, D148): R4, R42, R43 and P15 say what D142 to D144 decided and 4b3b21c, 0d339cc and 3076547 build, so no log format, stored data or committed figure changes. The set-aside rule by match moves no shipped verdict, for revision 16's reason. The take-back's lock re-check, the revision 17 default, is one check in T12 and changes no stored data.
1118:- Requirements (revision 23, D210): R4, R42, R43, R54 and P15 state two scope limits, so no code, log format, stored data, table or committed figure changes. 3ae10cc names more of a table that is not well formed than R4 now asks, and 43332c6 keeps every check it has under lock-file interference and interruption; nothing is removed.
1122:- Case log (revisions 6 to 15): nothing to migrate. The reader rule, the reader taking the lock first (revision 7), a check's shared hold (revision 8), and the head record before the first line (revision 9) change what a reader reports and when a writer waits or is refused. So do the gate and the lock-file scope (revision 10), the re-check before each step a reader reads (revision 11, D108; in R42 since revision 12), one save at a time (revision 13), the read-back and the repair's re-checks (revision 14), and the gate's re-check and the first head record linked into place (revision 15). None changes what the log or the head record holds. With D69's marks and D93's counts gone, the lock files hold no data. Since D95 each case folder also has a gate file, which the first writer creates before the case lock; no case folder exists outside tests, so none lacks one.
1131:- offline_lock in firm.yaml: on by default. Turning it off is a deliberate act that is recorded, and every evidence record states where data went.
1132:- show_policy_verdict in firm.yaml: on by default (D28). Off hides the outcome and every rule result in the workspace and the pack at once, without a release.
1143:3. Land the case store, the workspace, preview, pack and sign-off, and the offline lock:
1144:   - the case store has the case log in its stated scope, its gate and case lock, one save at a time, and the reader R43 describes since revision 12;
1146:   Run the P15 tests once on a Windows machine before the demo, because CI runs on Linux and checks share the gate and the lock there through a different call (D85). Demo on the owner's statements and on a synthetic case shaped like a business, both marked as demos (D23). The README and the trial sheet say case folders stay on a local disk with hard links (NTFS, APFS or a Linux file system, not FAT or exFAT), out of folders a sync, backup or cleanup tool manages, and that the lock files beside the log are the tool's own.
1148:5. Send the approver the exact data statement (D25): it runs on the firm's machine, the offline lock is on, and nothing leaves.
1166:- **The signal for the case log (revisions 5 to 21):** `cma case check` per case gives one of three outcomes. Intact: exit 0, only from a reader that found the case free. Being saved or not checked: exit 4, with the recorded entries that check out, nothing checked yet, or the lock error named. A bad entry: exit 3, with the first bad or incomplete entry by number, a recorded entry cut short, a lost head record or a log that two saves told recorded at one position left included. A writer refused because another holds the case says so ('another window or command is writing to this case') and writes nothing. A writer refused because a lock file, the gate or the case lock, was removed or replaced says so: 'a case lock file was removed or replaced while this window held the case', followed by 'nothing was written' and that the save was not recorded or, once any part of the line is written, 'part of its entry was written and the save was not recorded' (revision 19), or, refused at the head rename, 'its entry was written and the save was not recorded' (revision 20). No message about a save whose line is in the log says nothing was written. A save refused because another save is under way in the same window, or because the log changed since its check or while it was being recorded, says so and asks to reload. A save whose head record was not taken back after a failed read-back says that whether the save is in the log is not confirmed, names what stopped the take-back in its own words, and asks for the case log to be checked before anything else is saved (R42 since revision 17, D143; one answer since revision 21, D168). A save the file system failed names the error and says the save was not recorded or, when its head record was published and not taken back, that whether it is in the log is not confirmed (R42 since revision 18; one rule since revision 19). No message, and no error it carries, gives a second answer (revision 21). So does a save whose own check could not read the log, the head record or a lock file, and it never says the case changed or a lock file was removed or replaced (revision 20). If the friend sees the first with only one window open, or the second at all, something outside the tool is touching the case folder (a share, a forked process, a stuck command, a sync or backup client, a cleanup script), and the trial sheet says to move the case to a plain local folder.
1169:- **What fires when it does not work:** there is no remote alerting, by design. Instead the tool refuses, with a non-zero exit and a red banner, when reconciliation fails, the guard fails, the log chain breaks, a rule table fails validation, or a hosted model is requested under the offline lock. `cma report` exits non-zero if any case has traced figures below 100% or reconciled false, and `cma case check` exits 3 on a broken log.
1174:  - CI runs on Linux, so the Windows lock path is covered by Rollout step 3, not by CI.
1179:- **The lock files are not access control** (revisions 5 and 10). The gate and the case lock order honest writers on one machine; access is the case root's owner-only permissions (R31), which `cma case check` relies on rather than checks. Someone with the operating-system user's rights can delete the lock files. A writer then refuses once it sees either file gone or replaced (the gate since revision 15). The tool tries to show two saves told recorded at one position as a broken log, and to let a repair under lock-file interference remove only a save told it was not recorded (D124, with D125's read-back and re-check, R42 since revision 14); since revision 23 these are what it tries, not guarantees (D210). Such a person can also rewrite the log and its head record together, which the files alone cannot show. The sign-off and the pack pin the log head they saw (R22), so a later rewrite no longer matches a signed pack. A writer that keeps saving can keep other readers at being saved, but cannot make them report the log as intact or broken (revision 6); sign-off and every append judge the whole log while holding the case. A process that checks a case in a tight loop can make another window's save wait at the gate and then be refused (revisions 7 and 10); that takes the same user's rights, which already allow worse.
1180:- **Trust boundary:** bank data the applicant controls enters through the importers, and the loader checks its structure and balance chain. Descriptions are written by payers: they are escaped in HTML under a strict content security policy with no inline script, formula characters are neutralised in CSV, and they never go into prompts. The candidate rule ids shown for an ambiguous credit, and their buckets, come from the shipped tables and the two fixed profile kinds, not from payer text. Which wording is the bank's own code is listed in code, so neither a payer nor a table edit can claim it (D97). Case-log messages name entries by number and never quote their content.
1181:- **Secrets:** an optional model API key in the environment, only when the offline lock is off. It is never logged or written to evidence.
1195:  3. Someone turns the offline lock off and uses a hosted model. The prompt still carries only tables (P5), and the evidence records where data went.
1196:  4. Someone with the underwriter's login edits the case log and its head record together to hide an override. The files cannot show it, but a signed pack pins the head it saw, so the edit shows against every signed pack. Deleting the lock files and repairing the log while another window saves is meant to remove only a save that window was told was not recorded (D124, with D125's read-back and re-check, R42 since revision 14); since revision 23 that is what the tool tries, not a guarantee, and the signed pack's pinned head is what shows a lost save (D210).
1235:8. **MCP tool outputs (additions; refuse under the offline lock).** `rg -n 'compute_metrics|check_policy|transactions_for|statement_summary' -g '!.bridgie/**'`. Consumers: src/cma/mcp_server.py, tests/test_mcp_paths.py, and MCP clients outside the repo.
